# Rank 1262 / CVE-2024-28244: KaTeX's Parser bounds macro expansion by
# counting `MacroExpander.expansionCount` against `settings.maxExpand` on
# `this.gullet`, but the Unicode sub/superscript branch of
# parseSupSub -- src/Parser.js -- built the accumulated Unicode run into a
# plain string and handed it to a brand-new `new Parser(str, this.settings)`.
# That constructor creates its own MacroExpander, so the fresh instance's
# expansionCount starts at zero: nesting user-controlled macros inside a
# Unicode sub/superscript run resets the counter on every level, letting the
# expansion blow up exponentially past whatever maxExpand is configured to
# stop.
#
# The fixed revision does not touch the limit itself. It changes which instance keeps counting: the run is tokenized
# and handed to `this.subparse(subsupTokens)`, which pushes the tokens onto
# the current job's own `this.gullet` instead of spinning up a second one, so
# the existing expansionCount keeps accruing across the sub/superscript.
#
# No taint path connects a source to a sink here -- both the vulnerable and
# patched forms parse the same untrusted TeX string, and the control that
# changed is which object instance owns a resource counter, not a check on
# tainted data. That is the same shape logged ATTENTION for rank 1101
# (Faraday's unbounded nested-params recursion) and rank 1255 (kaml's
# anchor/alias billion-laughs): a bounded, counted operation is bypassed by
# recursing through a freshly constructed instance of the same class instead
# of continuing on the one whose counter is being enforced. The nearest
# concept is code.UnboundedResourceOperation, populated today only by
# bespoke per-language "Review" AST classifiers (Rust/Python/PHP/Ruby) plus a
# handful of narrow presence-node bindings (e.g. the axios
# maxContentLength/Buffer.concat binding in
# vyql/bindings/javascript/mfb63017a.vyql); none of them generalizes to "a
# class re-instantiates itself inside its own recursive-descent method,
# discarding the counter the outer instance enforces." Writing a binding
# on `new Parser(...)` inside Parser's own method would only ever match this
# file: Parser/MacroExpander are KaTeX-internal types with no public
# documentation surface, so nobody unfamiliar with this CVE would derive that
# binding from a library doc, which fails the genericity test in AGENTS.md.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1262: the Unicode sub/superscript run parses through a fresh Parser instance, resetting expansionCount"
  lang javascript
  file src/Parser.js
  reject_review code.UnboundedResourceOperation
  code
  ```
  export default class Parser {
      constructor(input, settings) {
          this.gullet = new MacroExpander(input, settings, this.mode);
          this.settings = settings;
      }

      parseSupSub(lex) {
          let str = uSubsAndSups[lex.text];
          const isSub = unicodeSubRegEx.test(lex.text);
          this.consume();
          while (true) {
              const token = this.fetch().text;
              if (!(uSubsAndSups[token])) { break; }
              if (unicodeSubRegEx.test(token) !== isSub) { break; }
              this.consume();
              str += uSubsAndSups[token];
          }
          // Now create a (sub|super)script.
          const body = (new Parser(str, this.settings)).parse();
          return body;
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1262: the Unicode sub/superscript run is subparsed on the current job's gullet, keeping expansionCount shared"
  lang javascript
  file src/Parser.js
  reject_review code.UnboundedResourceOperation
  code
  ```
  export default class Parser {
      constructor(input, settings) {
          this.gullet = new MacroExpander(input, settings, this.mode);
          this.settings = settings;
      }

      parseSupSub(lex) {
          const isSub = unicodeSubRegEx.test(lex.text);
          const subsupTokens = [];
          subsupTokens.push(new Token(uSubsAndSups[lex.text]));
          this.consume();
          while (true) {
              const token = this.fetch().text;
              if (!(uSubsAndSups[token])) { break; }
              if (unicodeSubRegEx.test(token) !== isSub) { break; }
              subsupTokens.unshift(new Token(uSubsAndSups[token]));
              this.consume();
          }
          // Now create a (sub|super)script.
          const body = this.subparse(subsupTokens);
          return body;
      }
  }
  ```
